            background: transparent !important;
            color: #fff !important;
        }

    .example-box {
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        border-radius: 8px;
        padding: 18px;
        margin-top: 15px;
        background-color: #f8f9fa;
    }

    .output-box {
        border-left: 4px solid #0d6efd;
        background-color: #f8f9fa;
        padding: 15px;
        margin-top: 15px;
        border-radius: 5px;
    }

    .note-box {
        border-left: 4px solid #ffc107;
        background-color: #fff8e1;
        padding: 15px;
        margin-top: 15px;
        border-radius: 5px;
    }

    table th,
    table td {
        vertical-align: middle;
    }
</style>

<main id="main" class="container py-5">

    <div class="row">

        <!-- Sidebar -->
        <div class="col-lg-3 mb-4">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/HTML/Views/Shared/HTMLTopics.cshtml")
        </div>

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <div class="col-lg-9">

            <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold mb-3">HTML Link Attributes</h1>

            <p class="lead">
                HTML link attributes control how hyperlinks behave, where they open,
                what information they provide, and how users interact with them.
            </p>

            <hr class="my-4" />

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">1. Introduction</h2>

            <p>
                The HTML <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> element creates hyperlinks.
                Attributes are added inside the opening <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag
                to control the behavior and additional information of the link.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="https://www.example.com"&gt;Visit Example&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                The most commonly used link attributes are:
                <code>href</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>title</code>,
                <code>download</code>, <code>rel</code>, and <code>id</code>.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">2. href Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>href</code> attribute specifies the destination URL of a hyperlink.
                It is the most important attribute of the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> element.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="https://www.google.com"&gt;Open Google&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>
            
            
            <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
<h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">
    Output
</h3>

                <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Result:</strong>
                <br />
                A link named <a href="https://www.google.com" target="_blank">Open Google</a>
                is displayed.
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">3. target Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>target</code> attribute specifies where the linked document
                should open.
            </p>

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped mt-3">
                <thead class="table-dark">
                    <tr>
                        <th>Value</th>
                        <th>Description</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>_self</code></td>
                        <td>Opens the link in the current tab. This is the default.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>_blank</code></td>
                        <td>Opens the link in a new tab or window.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>_parent</code></td>
                        <td>Opens the link in the parent browsing context.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>_top</code></td>
                        <td>Opens the link in the full browser window.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Example</h3>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="https://www.example.com" target="_blank"&gt;
    Open Example
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>
<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
<h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">
    Output
</h3>

                <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Result:</strong>
                <br />
                <a href="https://www.example.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    Open Example
                </a>
            </div>

            <div class="note-box mt-3">
                <strong>Note:</strong>
                When using <code>target="_blank"</code>, using
                <code>rel="noopener noreferrer"</code> is a common security practice.
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">4. title Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>title</code> attribute provides additional information about
                a link. Browsers commonly display its value as a tooltip when the user
                moves the mouse over the link.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="https://www.example.com"
   title="Visit Example Website"&gt;
    Example Website
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>




<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
<h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">
    Output
</h3>

                <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Result:</strong>
                <br />
                <a href="https://www.example.com"
                   target="_blank"
                   title="Visit Example Website">
                    Example Website
                </a>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">5. rel Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>rel</code> attribute defines the relationship between
                the current page and the linked resource.
            </p>

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped mt-3">
                <thead class="table-dark">
                    <tr>
                        <th>Value</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>noopener</code></td>
                        <td>Prevents the new page from accessing the original window through JavaScript.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>noreferrer</code></td>
                        <td>Prevents the browser from sending the referrer information.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>nofollow</code></td>
                        <td>Provides a hint to search engines not to pass ranking signals through the link.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>external</code></td>
                        <td>Indicates that the linked resource is external to the current site.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Example</h3>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="https://www.example.com"
   target="_blank"
   rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
    External Website
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">6. download Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>download</code> attribute tells the browser that the linked
                resource should be downloaded instead of simply opened.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="files/resume.pdf" download&gt;
    Download Resume
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Use Case:</strong>
                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    The download attribute is commonly used for PDFs, documents,
                    images, reports, and other downloadable resources.
                </p>
            </div>

            <h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Custom Download Filename</h3>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="files/resume.pdf" download="MyResume.pdf"&gt;
    Download Resume
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>
<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <p>
                The value of the <code>download</code> attribute can suggest a filename
                for the downloaded resource.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">7. id Attribute for Page Sections</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>id</code> attribute can be used on an element to create
                a target for a same-page link.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;h2 id="contact"&gt;Contact Us&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;a href="#contact"&gt;Go to Contact Section&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <p>
                The <code>#contact</code> part of the URL points to the element
                having <code>id="contact"</code>.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">8. Linking to Another Page in the Same Website</h2>

            <p>
                A relative URL can be used when linking to another page within
                the same website.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="about.html"&gt;About Us&lt;/a&gt;

&lt;a href="contact.html"&gt;Contact Us&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

            <div class="example-box">
                <p class="mb-2"><strong>Common Examples:</strong></p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>about.html</li>
                    <li>contact.html</li>
                    <li>products.html</li>
                    <li>services.html</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">9. Linking to a Specific Section</h2>

            <p>
                You can combine <code>href</code> and an element's
                <code>id</code> to navigate directly to a specific section.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="#services"&gt;View Services&lt;/a&gt;

&lt;h2 id="services"&gt;Our Services&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We provide web development services.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">10. Email Link</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>mailto:</code> scheme opens the user's default email application.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="mailto:info@example.com"&gt;
    Email Us
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
<h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">
    Output
</h3>

                <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>
                <br />
                <a href="mailto:info@example.com">Email Us</a>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">11. Telephone Link</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>tel:</code> scheme can be used to create a clickable
                telephone number, especially useful on mobile devices.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="tel:+919999999999"&gt;
    Call Us
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>
<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
<h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">
    Output
</h3>

                <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>
                <br />
                <a href="tel:+919999999999">Call Us</a>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">12. Complete Link Example</h2>

            <p>
                Multiple link attributes can be used together.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;a href="https://www.example.com"
   target="_blank"
   rel="noopener noreferrer"
   title="Visit Example Website"&gt;
    Visit Example
&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>
<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
<h3 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">
    Output
</h3>

                <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Result:</strong>
                <br />
                <a href="https://www.example.com"
                   target="_blank"
                   rel="noopener noreferrer"
                   title="Visit Example Website">
                    Visit Example
                </a>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">13. Common Link Attributes Summary</h2>

            <table class="table table-bordered table-hover mt-3">
                <thead class="table-dark">
                    <tr>
                        <th>Attribute</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>href</code></td>
                        <td>Specifies the destination</td>
                        <td><code>href="about.html"</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>target</code></td>
                        <td>Specifies where to open the link</td>
                        <td><code>target="_blank"</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>title</code></td>
                        <td>Provides additional information</td>
                        <td><code>title="About Us"</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>rel</code></td>
                        <td>Defines the relationship with the linked resource</td>
                        <td><code>rel="noopener"</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>download</code></td>
                        <td>Requests download of the resource</td>
                        <td><code>download</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>id</code></td>
                        <td>Creates an anchor target</td>
                        <td><code>id="contact"</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">14. Accessibility Considerations</h2>

            <p>
                Link text should clearly describe where the link leads.
                Avoid vague text such as "Click Here" when possible.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;!-- Less descriptive --&gt;
&lt;a href="courses.html"&gt;Click Here&lt;/a&gt;

&lt;!-- More descriptive --&gt;
&lt;a href="courses.html"&gt;View HTML Courses&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                Descriptive link text helps users understand the purpose of a link,
                including users who navigate websites with assistive technologies.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">15. Common Mistakes</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Forgetting the <code>href</code> attribute.</li>
                <li>Using incorrect or broken URLs.</li>
                <li>Using unclear link text.</li>
                <li>Using <code>target="_blank"</code> without considering <code>rel</code>.</li>
                <li>Using unnecessary attributes.</li>
                <li>Forgetting to use matching <code>id</code> values for page anchors.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">16. Practice Task</h2>

            <div class="example-box">
                <p><strong>Create an HTML page containing:</strong></p>

                <ol>
                    <li>A link to Google.</li>
                    <li>A link that opens in a new tab.</li>
                    <li>A link with a tooltip using the <code>title</code> attribute.</li>
                    <li>A downloadable PDF link.</li>
                    <li>An email link.</li>
                    <li>A telephone link.</li>
                    <li>A same-page link using an element <code>id</code>.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">17. Complete Practice Example</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;HTML Link Attributes&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;

    &lt;h1&gt;Link Examples&lt;/h1&gt;

    &lt;a href="https://www.google.com"
       target="_blank"
       rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
        Open Google
    &lt;/a&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;a href="files/sample.pdf" download&gt;
        Download PDF
    &lt;/a&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;a href="mailto:info@example.com"&gt;
        Email Us
    &lt;/a&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;a href="tel:+919999999999"&gt;
        Call Us
    &lt;/a&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;a href="#contact"&gt;
        Go to Contact
    &lt;/a&gt;

    &lt;h2 id="contact"&gt;Contact Section&lt;/h2&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Thank you for visiting our website.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
                <h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    HTML link attributes control how hyperlinks work and provide additional
                    information about linked resources. The most commonly used attributes are
                    <code>href</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>rel</code>,
                    <code>download</code>, and <code>id</code>. Understanding these attributes
                    helps you create useful, accessible, and well-structured navigation in HTML pages.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Navigation -->
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary"
                   href="@Url.Action("Hyperlinks", "LinksAndMedia", new { area = "HTML" })">
                    ← Previous: Hyperlinks
                </a>

                <a class="btn btn-primary"
                   href="@Url.Action("Images", "LinksAndMedia", new { area = "HTML" })">
                    Next: Images →
                </a>
            </div>

        </div>
    </div>
</main>